 The full tour. For setup, see the [README](../README.md).
 
-- **Web access.** Menu → **Web access** configures search providers, API keys, quick/standard/deep routing chains, provider modes, extraction order, and enabled operations. Pi can search, read public pages, crawl with Tavily, and explicitly start research jobs. Fallback stays within the selected tier. Request counters record this installation's observed usage by provider, operation, mode, and purpose. [Configuration and limitations](web-access.md).
-
 - **Durable runs.** Every model request, tool call, queued message, and subagent is a durable task in one SQLite file. Kill the server mid-run and start it again: the run continues. A tool call that was cut off reruns if it is safe to; otherwise the model is told it was interrupted.
 - **Themes.** A theme changes the app's look and layout without a change to its code: a folder of CSS, fonts, images, and optional colors (`docs/extensions/themes.md`). Appearance has a **Theme** row above **Colors**: **Pocket**, the app's own look; **Lantern**, a calm, warm dark theme that comes with the app, made for reading for hours (warm brown-grey with warm off-white text and a muted amber accent, one readable column of about 72 characters, tool calls as one quiet line until opened, and your messages set off by a quiet amber bar instead of cards); and each theme folder. A theme stays when the colors change; one with colors of its own brings them when you choose it. The owner can **Duplicate** the theme or the colors in use into a folder of their own (in the data folder's `themes/`), **Edit with Pi** (a session in the theme's folder, with a message ready that names the guide), and **Remove** one (its folder moves to `themes/.removed/`). Each save shows at once in every tab that uses the theme, without a reload, and a reload never flashes the app's own look. A theme loads only its own files; one that breaks a rule keeps its last good version, and the owner is told what and where. Each person chooses for each browser, guests too, and the owner can make the theme and colors in use the default for browsers that have not chosen. The launcher's Themes and `/theme <name>` choose themes as well as colors. A theme can bring a pair of palettes, dark and light, that follows the system's setting. Safe mode (`?noext=1`) loads no theme.
 - **Omarchy colors.** All of Omarchy's themes, from Tokyo Night and Gruvbox to Rosé Pine and the light ones, drawn from each theme's own `colors.toml`, in soft tiles: rounded windows and controls on 1px hairlines, soft shadows only on what floats, Geist for words and JetBrains Mono for code, paths, and numbers (both bundled in `web/fonts/` under the SIL Open Font License); light themes too. Menu → **Appearance** (or the **Theme** tile, the launcher, or `/theme gruvbox`) picks one under **Colors**, as well as a theme folder's `colors.toml` (any Omarchy theme folder dropped into `themes/` shows there); the mini-desktop cards in Appearance preview a theme on hover or keyboard focus, and leaving without choosing restores the saved theme. The new look grows as a circle from where you tapped. **Desktop** follows the Omarchy theme of the machine Pi Pocket runs on: change your desktop's theme and the app follows within seconds, optionally with your wallpaper in the gaps.
